## The runtime

This build runs on no engine, so the layer beneath the game is yours as well:
the frame loop and the delta time it hands each update, fitting the fixed
logical stage onto the canvas, the keyboard and the input actions the player
drives the game with, the audio including the cues that loop, loading the
sprites and sounds you produce, and the on-screen debug overlay. The
specification in `specs/` states what Wick needs from that layer and fixes
nothing else about it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
## The {{engine.name}} engine

This build runs on the **{{engine.name}}** engine, installed as a dependency of
the project, so import it by its bare package name; there is nothing to add to
`package.json` for it and nothing to install.

The engine's own documentation is at `{{engine.docs}}`. It is the authoritative
description of the engine's API; the specification in `specs/` describes the
game and does not restate the engine. The documentation also carries a complete
worked example of unit-testing a game built on it.

Build the game on the engine rather than beside it. The engine owns the frame
loop and the delta time it hands your update, the input actions, the audio cue
bus, asset loading, and the on-screen debug overlay. It also holds the game's
state, by value: each frame it hands `update` the current state read-only and
keeps what `update` returns as the next, so a frame builds the next state from
the current one rather than writing into it. Register what the game needs with
the engine and use what it gives back. What remains yours is the game itself:
its simulation and everything it draws.
{{else}}
## The {{engine.name}} engine

This build runs on the **{{engine.name}}** engine, installed as a dependency of
the project, so import it by its bare package name; there is nothing to add to
`package.json` for it and nothing to install.

The engine's own documentation is at `{{engine.docs}}`. It is the authoritative
description of the engine's API; the specification in `specs/` describes the
game and does not restate the engine.

Build the game inside the engine's framework rather than beside it. The engine
owns the frame loop and the delta time it hands each tick, rendering and the
camera, the input actions, the audio cue bus, asset loading, and the on-screen
debug overlay, and the game is written as subclasses of its classes: a game
instance, a game mode, actors and their components, and the controllers that
drive them. The engine constructs, ticks, and renders them in a fixed order.
Register what the game needs with the engine and use what it gives back. What
remains yours is the game itself: its rules, its state, and everything it draws
through the engine's pipeline.
{{/if}}

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Produce an implementation that matches the
specification exactly. `specs/overview.md` states what you implement, which
files stay as they are, the code quality the build is held to, and the commands
that run over the finished repository.

## The build interface

`npm ci` then `npm run build` must produce the static site, with no other
manual step, into `dist/` at the project root, with an `index.html` at the root
of that directory. Serving that directory as-is must run the game, both at the
root of a static file server and mounted under a sub-path, so every URL the
build emits or constructs at runtime is page-relative. The scripts that do this
are already in `package.json`.

## The assets the game plays

Wick ships no art and no sound, and this build makes both: the lamplighter, the
thirteen enemies, the death puff, the sixteen weapon effects, the twenty-seven
icons, the gems, the pickups, the ground tile, and the game's cues and music.
`specs/assets.md` states what to produce, which tool on this machine's `PATH`
produces each file, where each lands under `assets/`, how it is loaded, and the
bar each is held to. It also lists what stays drawn in code.

## Driving the game in a browser

The project's Playwright and its headless Chromium are installed, so the built
game can be served and driven in a real browser.

When you launch the browser, use the project's own Playwright: import it from
your project (for example `import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the
project-local CLI with `npx playwright`. There is no global Playwright on
`PATH`; it lives in your project's `node_modules` like any other dependency.
Launch Chromium with the `--no-sandbox` argument, since this container has no
Chromium sandbox and a default launch fails.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a git repository, initialized before you started with the
starter files in its first commit. Your finished implementation is published
directly from this repository's commits, so work you leave uncommitted is not
published. When the build is complete, commit everything: run `git add -A` then
`git commit`, and confirm `git status` reports a clean working tree with nothing
uncommitted or untracked. A `.gitignore` already excludes `node_modules/`, the
build output, and coverage reports, so a clean tree means committing your
source, your tests, your `package-lock.json`, your produced files under
`assets/`, and your `README.md`.
